WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.790
But in North Carolina, the election results
were really weird.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:13.220
These squares represent all the voters in
North Carolina.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.200
They were voting for these 13 seats in Congress.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.610
About half voted for Republicans.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.390
And about 48 percent voted for Democrats.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:27.100
So you might think, of the 13 Congressional
seats, maybe Democrats would've won 6 seats,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.880
and Republicans would've won 7.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.050
But no.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.070
Democrats only won 3 of 13 seats — way less
than half.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.950
This imbalance was because North Carolina's
Congressional districts had been "gerrymandered."

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.000
It means that these voters had been grouped
into districts very strategically

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.640
with the goal of benefiting one party.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.780
Gerrymandering has pretty much always happened
in America.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:55.340
That's because, every 10 years the political districts
are redrawn.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.560
And in most cases, those new lines get drawn

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.320
by whoever holds power in state governments at the time.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.740
That's what happened in 2010.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:08.659
Republicans won control of lots of state governments,
and redrew the political lines to favor themselves.

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:13.350
And over the next few years, redistricting
helped them hold onto almost all those states.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.040
This shifted the balance of power.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.100
And it turns out that behind a lot of this,
was one guy.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.440
This is Thomas Hofeller:

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.240
The mapmaker who helped Republicans gerrymander
districts over the last decade.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:41.960
When Hofeller died in 2018, his daughter found
thousands of his emails and files, which she

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.439
shared with activists.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.640
The files show that Tom Hoeffler’s fingerprints
are all over the way America’s political

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:50.029
maps look today.

00:01:50.029 --> 00:01:53.079
But North Carolina was his masterpiece.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.840
And if you want to understand why gerrymandering
is a such a big problem in the US,

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.360
that’s a good place to start.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.000
The basics of gerrymandering are actually
pretty simple.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:09.629
If you're a Republican trying to keep power,
you want to do two things.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:14.870
First, "pack" as many Democratic voters as
possible into a single district.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.349
If you have a district where almost everyone
votes Democrat, that means almost half of

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:22.570
those votes are basically wasted.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:26.040
You can also "crack" big Democratic areas
into separate districts — where there are

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.629
slightly more Republicans.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:33.129
So even though an area has a lot of Democratic
votes, they would actually lose in this district

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:35.769
and in this district.

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:40.900
These are the two elements of classic gerrymandering:
Packing and cracking.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.359
And Hoffeler employed these techniques masterfully
in North Carolina.

00:02:45.359 --> 00:02:48.370
In 2011 he was hired to redraw the state's
political lines.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.320
And for congressional districts, he came up
with this map.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.180
Now, I just want to focus in on District 12,
this weird skinny shape.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.340
In order to make sense of this shape, we have
to look at another map.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.829
This map shows the percentage of black people
in each neighborhood.

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:07.670
The bluer areas are where more black people
live.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:11.909
Hofeller basically gathered up black people
in Winston-Salem, Greensboro, and Charlotte

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:14.999
— and packed them into one district.

00:03:14.999 --> 00:03:17.549
So that's how District 12 happened.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:22.959
Hoeffler also did this with North Carolina’s
state representatives and state senators.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.610
For example here are the state senate districts.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:29.150
Here, he packed Winston-Salem into one district.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:33.260
And then packed Greensboro into its own district.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.680
These new districts helped Republicans get
a stranglehold on power in the North Carolina statehouse.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.320
And over the next few years, they were able to pass crucial legislation.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.240
A strict new voter ID law in North Carolina.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.820
Which bathrooms transgender individuals can use in North Carolina.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:55.820
In 2016 and 2017, federal courts ruled both
of these maps were unconstitutional.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:59.560
They said what North Carolina Republicans
did wasn't just gerrymandering — it was

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.820
racial gerrymandering, done to deliberately
dilute the political power of black people.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.900
The courts said that the Republicans in the
North Carolina statehouse now had to redraw

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.400
the lines without looking at racial demographics.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.320
So they went back... to Tom Hofeller.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.920
This time, Hofeller couldn't look at race.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:22.030
Instead, he looked at which areas voted for
Democrats and which areas voted for Republicans.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.099
Instead of a racial gerrymander, it would
be a partisan gerrymander.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:29.220
Here's that map, using data from 2014.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.020
The bluer an area, the more Democratic voters
there are.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:36.650
Now, if you zoom in here, to Greensboro, you
can see one of the highest concentrations

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.389
of Democratic voters in the state.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:44.069
Hoffeler drew a congressional district line
to crack this community in half.

00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:47.960
This meant Democrats here, were now the minority
in their district.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:51.610
And Democrats here were also the minority
in their district.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.400
Hoffeler employed these techniques all over
the state to create North Carolina’s new

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.800
political districts.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.140
And the first big test for these new maps
would be the 2018 election.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.150
Democrats were expected to turn out in droves.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.160
Democrats are vying for a potential blue wave.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.700
The wave that Republicans fear is going to wipe them out.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:13.040
So, how did the maps do?

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.820
For state representatives, Democrats got 51
percent of the vote.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.490
They won only 46 percent of seats.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.949
For state senate, they received half the vote
— and won just 42 percent of seats.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:28.280
And for Congress?

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.210
You already know how that one turned out.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:36.470
Democrats won nearly half the votes — but
won only three out of 13 seats.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:40.669
A year later, in 2019, the Supreme Court weighed
in.

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:45.099
They said it was beyond their reach… that
it wasn't their job to fix it.

00:05:45.099 --> 00:05:48.700
All of this raised an existential question:

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.539
If Republicans could continue drawing the
lines to stay in power, how could they ever

00:05:52.539 --> 00:05:56.620
be elected out of office?

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:02.419
But the Supreme Court ruling left open the
possibility for state courts to rule on partisan

00:06:02.419 --> 00:06:03.870
gerrymandering.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:08.800
And in September 2019, that's exactly what
North Carolina's Supreme Court did.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.830
The court found that partisan gerrymandering
violated the state constitution.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:16.331
In the court’s decision, it was Hofeller's
files that helped prove that North Carolina

00:06:16.331 --> 00:06:20.550
Republicans drew these lines with the clear
intention of benefiting themselves.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.620
Ultimately, the court said North Carolina
Republicans had to redraw the state house

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.979
and state senate maps one more time.

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:32.990
This new map approved by North Carolina legislators
is much less biased toward one party — even

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.360
though it took some extreme measures, and
nearly a decade, to force politicians to draw

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.870
a fairer map.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:43.810
In the last few years, the courts in several
states, like Florida and Pennsylvania, have

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.419
made partisan gerrymandering much harder.

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:50.300
And now that's also the case in North Carolina.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.919
Hofeller is gone now.

00:06:52.919 --> 00:06:57.080
But in other states across the country, many
maps he helped draw are still in use.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:02.690
And while there's now a clearer strategy to
challenge those maps in state courts...

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:07.180
… many voters are still, effectively, not
choosing their representatives.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:12.840
It’s like Hoeffler said:, the representatives
are choosing the voters.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.610
"... of course, redistricting is democracy
at work.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.740
Redistricting is like an election in reverse.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.610
It's a great event."

